# Transverse-field Gibbs memory with a uniform computational bias

**Primary-runner role: source integrity only.** The proposed 60-second primary checks the three exact mathematical bodies, one canonical consumer, selected recovery evidence and an actual root binding. It performs no physical simulation, mathematical proof test, memory preparation or audit certification. It remains unexecuted in this source-only proposal and fails closed until root supplies a separate actual binding and every declared receipt input.

A finite graph with diagonal occupation interactions and onsite transverse X/Y fields can have a computational Gibbs diagonal whose every full one-bit conditional is uniformly below one half, even with signed, spatially frustrated edges. A strict local longitudinal margin, a positive inverse-temperature lower bound and a finite transverse-strength upper bound give the explicit bound below, independent of the graph size and of the probability of the conditioned computational record. On the inherited fixed memory ray this supplies the local-bias consumer's reversible compression input. A physical non-diagonal source-removal stage additionally requires its full reduced Gibbs density, or a full reduced trace-distance delivery promise. Every original factor, displaced record, reference and garbage factor remains in the comparison target.

The exact root proposal 5cbafbd7, independently sealed derivation 5f716396 and subsequent exposed comparison 6693f24d are preserved below byte for byte. Actual root source acceptance 41b73666 is archived with its verification, seal and real metadata receipt. The source-only wrapper records their accepted scope; it does not rewrite their historical proposed/unreviewed labels or imply that a primary, cache, mutation, graph, repository binding, PR or retained audit has run.

## Finite Hamiltonian and the sharp uniform conditional bound

Let B consist of L>=1 qubits on a finite ordinary-nearest-neighbor graph. Write n_i=(I-Z_i)/2 and use the actual supplied coefficients

    G = sum_i h_i n_i + sum_e(J_e n_i n_j+c_e I) + k0 I
        + sum_i(g_i I-a_i X_i-b_i Y_i),
    g_i=sqrt(a_i^2+b_i^2),
    c_e>=max(0,-J_e),  k0>=0,
    h_i-sum_(e incident i)max(0,-J_e)>=gamma>0,
    beta>=beta_min>0,  beta finite,  0<=g_i<=g_max<infinity.

Each displayed block is positive. The field-margin condition in particular implies h_i>0. Set tau_G=exp(-beta G)/Tr exp(-beta G), p(x)=<x|tau_G|x>, R_i=sqrt(gamma^2+4g_i^2), and R_max=sqrt(gamma^2+4g_max^2). Every computational record y on B without site i has positive probability, and

    p(x_i=1 | x_without_i=y)
      <= q_i = [1-(gamma/R_i)tanh(beta R_i/2)]/2
      <= q_star = [1-(gamma/R_max)tanh(beta_min R_max/2)]/2 < 1/2.

The one-qubit member h=gamma, g=g_max, beta=beta_min attains q_star, so this uniform allowance is sharp for the stated class. The result concerns the B marginal in this basis. It does not bound conditionals after measuring an arbitrary retained reference or after changing the measurement basis. Neither product input, spatial mixing, unfrustrated edges, full-output independence nor a Markov law is assumed.

A local Z phase transformation makes each onsite transverse coefficient real nonnegative while preserving computational probabilities. After removing a scalar for the probability calculation, the finite product formula has a positive closed-path expansion. Condition on the entire temporal paths of all sites except i. The remaining site is a ferromagnetic temporal ring with every longitudinal field at least the field corresponding to gamma. An elementary common-uniform heat-bath coupling compares it to the constant-field ring. Averaging over the other paths conditioned on the specified endpoint record preserves the inequality. At each fixed finite L the positive-denominator ratio converges to the exact Gibbs conditional, and the constant-field ring converges to the one-qubit formula. Zero transverse fields follow by constant paths or fixed-volume continuity. This proof uses temporal order, not a spatial ferromagnetic assumption, an efficient sampler or a uniform finite-product rate for rare records. The elementary comparison process supplies no physical equilibration timescale.

The independent derivation was sealed before the candidate was opened, but both derivations use this same mathematical mechanism and the same selected model/effort with shared older sources. Their agreement is not independent-method replication or a formal proof certificate. The later comparison is explicitly source-exposed.

## Actual-member calibration and a concrete finite ray family

A sufficient local interval certificate is

    h_i^0-epsilon_i-sum_(e incident i)max(0,-J_e^0+zeta_e)>=gamma,
    |a_i-a_i^0|<=u_i, |b_i-b_i^0|<=v_i,
    sqrt[(|a_i^0|+u_i)^2+(|b_i^0|+v_i)^2]<=g_max.

The scalar reserves must separately cover every admitted member: c_e>=max(0,-J_e) and k0>=0 throughout the class; a fixed transverse reserve t_i must satisfy t_i>=g_i. A bound on nonconstant coefficients alone does not certify these reserves. Effective finite parameter selection uses supplied computable bounds, including a certified rational q_bar with q_star<=q_bar<1/2; arbitrary inaccessible real data are not a control algorithm.

For a degree-at-most-two ray, h_i in [3/2,5/2], J_e in [-1/4,1/4], c_e=1/4, g_i<=1/4, k0=0 and finite beta>=1 give gamma=1 and the uniform rational choice q_bar=3/8. This includes nonzero onsite transverse fields and nonzero diagonal pair interactions that do not commute. The source norm allowance is at most (7L-1)/2. These are a supplied finite mathematical class and an upper allowance, not inferred native couplings or equality for every member.

This interval result certifies the actual member's equilibrium diagonal. It does not approximate a fixed nominal global state, coherent quench unitary, physical scalar energy, work or raw moments. A fixed nominal dynamical target still needs its own full-space residual bound. As beta_min gamma tends to zero or g_max/gamma grows without bound, the certified margin can vanish; this is a limitation of the quantified certificate, not a general impossibility theorem.

